{"requirements":[{"id":"primary-role","requirement":"node1 operates as the writable PostgreSQL primary.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 directly reports that it is not in recovery, has transaction_read_only=off, and successfully commits and reads the probe row.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6c984884fb7c4b8d","cmd-320ca067744746f5"]},{"id":"standby-roles","requirement":"node2 and node3 operate as read-only streaming standbys.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes report recovery mode and read-only transactions, have active WAL receivers using distinct slots, and explicitly reject INSERT operations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-35f65b3bcc9b4941","cmd-9543d6d463644aac","cmd-6d03812cb91e4bb3","cmd-7ee71bb67a7e4221"]},{"id":"committed-replication","requirement":"Committed changes on node1 replicate to both standbys.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The row committed on node1 appears on both standbys with the same values and timestamp. Final primary observations show both streams active with zero replay lag; synchronous remote-apply acknowledgements are configured for both.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6c984884fb7c4b8d","cmd-35f65b3bcc9b4941","cmd-9543d6d463644aac","cmd-9ccc840824f748de","cmd-320ca067744746f5"]},{"id":"network-separation","requirement":"PostgreSQL replication uses the dedicated database network and PostgreSQL traffic stays off the management network.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"PostgreSQL listens only on 172.31.90.x overlay addresses, and TCP/5432 does not accept connections on the 10.100.13.x management addresses. However, the 172.31.90.x routes are IPIP tunnels whose local and remote endpoints are those management addresses, so the evidence shows logical endpoint isolation but not that replication traffic avoids the management network as its underlay.","evidence_ids":["cmd-713e5098a08946a5","cmd-a18f5808b8fa4b1b","cmd-b0f13169652f4d82","cmd-b484b41076b14076","cmd-406f27c6dd074340","cmd-8885ffec59b04b6d","cmd-c64e82fa6f714579","cmd-b0f4c0e0f3204bff","cmd-ffdaac4dc048451d","cmd-320ca067744746f5"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"All three nodes retain healthy package state and no failed services, and the new listeners are narrowly bound to the database-overlay addresses. The only observed executor residue is a small standby write-test log left in /tmp on node2 and node3.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The PostgreSQL role topology and end-to-end replication behavior are directly demonstrated: node1 is writable, both standbys are read-only, and a committed change reaches both. The network contract is only partially met because PostgreSQL endpoints are isolated behind overlay addresses while the overlay itself traverses the management-address network.","limitations":["Only one committed probe transaction was observed; sustained replication was not tested.","No restart or reboot transition was captured, so enabled-state checks do not directly prove post-reboot recovery.","No packet capture was provided; the network-path assessment relies on the observed IPIP tunnel endpoints, routes, replication client addresses, and listener bindings."]}
